SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Marcos, Duterte proclaimed winners in PH’s fastest vote count","publisher":"Philippine News Agency","publication_date":"2022-05-25","url":"https://www.pna.gov.ph/index.php/articles/1175224","claim":"Congress, sitting as the National Board of Canvassers, proclaimed Ferdinand Marcos Jr. president-elect on May 25, 2022 after the fastest canvassing in Philippine election history.","locator":"article body and headline; proclamation passage","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government newswire reporting the formal proclamation and vote count.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly precedes Marcos's entry into office in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Marks the lawful transfer into Marcos's presidency; not proof of democratic quality by itself.","contrary_evidence":["A formal proclamation does not establish that the campaign was fully fair or uncontested."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Marcos Jr. sworn in as PH's 17th president","publisher":"Philippine News Agency","publication_date":"2022-06-30","url":"https://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1177892","claim":"Marcos took his oath as the 17th president on June 30, 2022 and promised to promote unity and bring progress to the country.","locator":"headline and opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government wire with a dated inauguration report.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year and the start of Marcos's term.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos's assumption of office and acceptance of constitutional transfer.","contrary_evidence":["The inauguration shows succession, not necessarily democratic restraint in day-to-day rule."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Final Report: 2022 General Elections in the Philippines","publisher":"The Carter Center","publication_date":"2024-03-15","url":"https://cartercentee50c07c05.blob.core.windows.net/blobcartercentee50c07c05/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/philippines-final-report-050922.pdf","claim":"The Carter Center said voters and domestic observer groups had general confidence in COMELEC and in candidates' willingness to accept results, but interlocutors raised concerns about disinformation, media freedom, political-economic links, and the transparency and reliability of the automated system.","locator":"pp. 3-4 / PDF lines 77-89","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent election expert mission with a full report on the 2022 elections.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on the May 9, 2022 election, directly relevant to Marcos's accession.","ruler_attribution":"This is mostly about the election environment Marcos inherited and won within, not his later governing conduct.","contrary_evidence":["The report is retrospective and not all concerns are uniquely attributable to Marcos personally."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.6","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Final Report: 2022 General Elections in the Philippines","publisher":"The Carter Center","publication_date":"2024-03-15","url":"https://cartercentee50c07c05.blob.core.windows.net/blobcartercentee50c07c05/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/philippines-final-report-050922.pdf","claim":"The Center found a higher incidence of machine problems in the 2022 elections that were locally disruptive and also found that observed precinct procedures did not effectively ensure the secrecy of the vote.","locator":"pp. 4-5 / PDF lines 123-142","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent technical election assessment with specific process findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022 election process.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly institutional/electoral-system context; relevant to Marcos's accession but not a personal action by him.","contrary_evidence":["The report says the problems did not jeopardize overall integrity, so this is a mixed rather than catastrophic finding."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Final Report: 2022 General Elections in the Philippines","publisher":"The Carter Center","publication_date":"2024-03-15","url":"https://cartercentee50c07c05.blob.core.windows.net/blobcartercentee50c07c05/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/philippines-final-report-050922.pdf","claim":"The Carter Center said campaign-finance rules were only partly effective, with spending caps widely viewed as too low, a mostly unregulated pre-campaign period, and weak enforcement capacity at COMELEC.","locator":"pp. 6-7 / PDF lines 157-173 and 1811-1838","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent election report with detailed legal-analysis findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022 campaign and election cycle.","ruler_attribution":"This concerns the campaign system that enabled Marcos's victory, not a personal order by him after inauguration.","contrary_evidence":["The report identifies system-wide loopholes, not necessarily a Marcos-only manipulation scheme."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.5","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"NAMFREL 2022 National and Local Elections Preliminary Report","publisher":"NAMFREL","publication_date":"2022-07-18","url":"https://namfrel.org.ph/2022/2022nle.htm","claim":"NAMFREL said the 2022 elections went smoothly but not without issues, and it separately recorded pockets of violence and intimidation during election day.","locator":"report page entries dated July 18 and May 9, 2022","source_type":"civil_society","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Major Philippine election watchdog, but the accessible page is a portal to the report summary rather than the full PDF text.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022 election cycle.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly about the election environment rather than Marcos's later conduct.","contrary_evidence":["The accessible summary is limited and does not by itself resolve the full range of election disputes."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Philippines | 2022 Digital News Report","publisher":"Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism, University of Oxford","publication_date":"2022-06-15","url":"https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/digital-news-report/2022/philippines","claim":"The report said concerns over press freedom grew because Marcos held professional journalists in low regard during the campaign; it also said he barred journalists from campaign events, snubbed debates, and favored friendly influencers and a partisan TV station.","locator":"lines 32-40","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent research report with direct, dated observations of campaign behavior and media environment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in the 2022 campaign period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Marcos's own campaign conduct and the surrounding information environment.","contrary_evidence":["This is a media-research country note, not an election tribunal finding."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement of the Commission on Human Rights on the attempt to trivialize and justify the dangers of red-tagging","publisher":"Commission on Human Rights, Philippines","publication_date":"2022-04-11","url":"https://chr.gov.ph/statement-of-the-commission-on-human-rights-on-the-attempt-to-trivialize-and-justify-the-dangers-of-red-tagging/","claim":"CHR said red-tagging in recent years has led to vilification, surveillance, harassment, unlawful arrests, enforced disappearance, and killings, and it warned that government officials continued to resort to the practice.","locator":"lines 91-103","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Philippine constitutional human-rights body, dated and explicit about the harms and continuing government use.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 campaign and transition period.","ruler_attribution":"The statement addresses state-linked practice during the Marcos transition, though not every incident is personally ordered by Marcos.","contrary_evidence":["CHR is a rights-monitoring institution, so the statement is protective rather than neutral."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.7","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PBBM urges Congress to pass admin’s 19 priority measures","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/news_releases/pbbm-urges-congress-to-pass-admins-19-priority-measures/","claim":"Marcos asked Congress to pass National Government Rightsizing, Budget Modernization, E-Government, and E-Governance measures, saying they would improve service delivery, institutional capacity, and fiscal discipline.","locator":"lines 41-55","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government release summarizing a first-term SONA policy program.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and ruler-authored.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Marcos policy agenda, though it is legislative proposal rather than enacted reform.","contrary_evidence":["These are reform proposals, not completed institutional-strengthening outcomes."],"lenses":["4B.4","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Philippines still among worst countries in prosecuting killers of journalists - CPJ","publisher":"GMA News Online","publication_date":"2022-11-03","url":"https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/topstories/nation/850168/philippines-still-among-worst-countries-in-prosecuting-killers-of-journalists-cpj/story/","claim":"CPJ said the Philippines remained 7th among the worst countries for prosecuting killers of journalists, with 14 unsolved journalist murders, and warned that impunity could continue under Marcos after the killing of Percy Lapid.","locator":"article body and quoted CPJ report summary","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent local report on a major press-freedom index and its Marcos-era implications.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and after the Percy Lapid killing.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects the Marcos-era environment, though the murders themselves are not all directly attributed to him.","contrary_evidence":["The DOJ response quoted in the article shows at least some reactive state enforcement, but not a resolved structural fix."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Disinformation, red-tagging of opposition lawmakers in PH 'increased alarmingly' ahead of 2022 elections - APHR","publisher":"GMA News Online","publication_date":"2023-03-05","url":"https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/topstories/nation/862836/disinformation-red-tagging-of-opposition-lawmakers-in-ph-increased-alarmingly-ahead-of-2022-elections-a/story/","claim":"APHR said disinformation and red-tagging of opposition lawmakers increased alarmingly ahead of the May 2022 polls, and that Leni Robredo was the prime target of intensified disinformation and red-tagging in social media.","locator":"headline and opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Regional parliamentary rights network reported through a major local outlet, but it is retrospective and third-party summarized.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on the 2022 election period.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly about the campaign environment that benefited Marcos, not a direct post-inauguration order by him.","contrary_evidence":["Retrospective and coalition-based, so it should not be treated as a direct legal finding."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"ACLED: The communist insurgency in the Philippines: A 'protracted people’s war' continues","publisher":"ACLED","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://acleddata.com/report/communist-insurgency-philippines-protracted-peoples-war-continues","claim":"ACLED recorded nearly 50 red-tagging related violent events targeting civilians between 2020 and June 30, 2023, including 13 events after Marcos took power on June 30, 2022.","locator":"report text and footnote 47-48 discussion","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Event-data organization using transparent methodology; retrospective but concrete about post-inauguration continuation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Spans the target year and the immediate follow-on period, so it is retrospective context rather than pure 2022 contemporaneous reporting.","ruler_attribution":"The events are not all personally ordered by Marcos, but they show continuity of the red-tagging environment after he assumed office.","contrary_evidence":["The report covers a broader period than 2022 alone."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.9","4B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I screened roughly 30 documents across PNA, PCO, the Carter Center, NAMFREL, Reuters Institute, CHR, GMA, Philstar, ACLED, ICHRP, COMELEC, and related election-law materials.
- I kept election-process reports, press-freedom and red-tagging evidence, and a small set of official Marcos agenda/transfer documents.
- I rejected most foreign-policy, economic, and generic governance pieces because they did not bear directly on political freedom.
- I rejected several duplicate campaign-disinformation items when they repeated the same Marcos/media issue already covered better by the Reuters Institute, Carter Center, or CHR.
- I excluded later 2024-2026 material unless it was a retrospective report about the 2022 period, as with ACLED or the Carter Center final report.
- I did not rely on the client matrix, and I did not use the 2022 nuclear or international-peace material except where it had no bearing on political freedom.

**Remaining gaps**
- I still do not have a clean, direct Marcos order or directive showing him personally tightening or loosening institutional constraints beyond agenda-setting, appointments, and campaign behavior.
- The strongest favorable evidence is mostly about the formal transfer of power and policy proposals, not proven democratic deepening.
- The strongest adverse evidence is concentrated in the campaign/information environment and red-tagging, not in a full institutional-capture record.
- More search could help on one narrow point only: whether any 2022 cabinet or COMELEC-facing action by Marcos materially improved media access, election transparency, or the independence of oversight bodies.
